/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  equil04_reforming_carbon_declared_pathways  --  a reformer that cokes at
                                                  equilibrium, and a declared
                                                  list that says it does not.

  The third of the declared-pathways witnesses (equil02 methanol, equil03
  ammonia oxidation), and the one with a SOLID.  Methane and steam at a
  steam-to-carbon ratio of 1.0, 900 K, 1 bar, ideal gas, go to three
  equilibrium reactors side by side; every gas answer is read as MOLE
  FRACTIONS of the fluid and every solid answer per mole of methane fed, so
  the split is bookkeeping.

    gibbs     gibbsReactor over EVERY species (CH4 water CO CO2 H2
              graphite): no reaction declared, the minimum of G under the
              C, H and O balances, graphite given its own pure-solid phase.
    complete  equilibriumReactor over a COMPLETE set of independent
              reactions for six species over three elements (6 - 3 = 3):
                  steamReforming    CH4 + H2O  <->  CO + 3 H2
                  waterGasShift     CO + H2O   <->  CO2 + H2
                  methaneCracking   CH4        <->  C(gr) + 2 H2
              Boudouard (2 CO <-> C(gr) + CO2) is NOT added: it is
              cracking - reforming + shift, and a fourth reaction would be
              refused as linearly dependent.  Declaring it IN PLACE of
              cracking gives the same outlet -- an equilibrium does not care
              which route reaches it.
    declared  equilibriumReactor over reforming and the shift ONLY:
              Boudouard and methane cracking are left OUT, and with them
              every pathway to carbon.

  THE PREMISE IS YOURS.  Leaving the carbon routes out states that, over
  the catalyst this reactor carries, carbon does not form at these
  conditions.  That is kinetic knowledge; the engine cannot check it and does
  not try.  It does NOT say carbon cannot form thermodynamically -- the
  outlet says the opposite, and says how much the premise is asserting.

  WHAT THE THREE ANSWERS SHOW (measured with this case, 2026-09-27):
    gibbs      graphite DEPOSITS: 0.2168 mol per mol of methane fed (21.7 %
               of the carbon).  The solid-free gas had carbon activity
               a = 1.580 > 1, so it was not the minimum of G.  Gas left:
               y_CH4 0.1146, y_H2O 0.1374, y_CO 0.0923, y_CO2 0.0476,
               y_H2 0.6081.
    complete   the SAME outlet, gas and graphite, to the printed digits
               (a solid participant: absent in the first solve, activity
               1.580 read off it, then present at activity 1).
    declared   NO graphite, and the gas is the solid-free equilibrium:
               y_CH4 0.1568, y_H2O 0.1056, y_CO 0.1204, y_CO2 0.0512,
               y_H2 0.5660.  Its carbon activity is published,
               activity_graphite = 1.580: the outlet is SUPERSATURATED in
               carbon, a metastable state the declared list asserts.
               Recompute it by hand, by either route the list leaves out:
                 a_C = Kp_crack * p_CH4 / p_H2^2      (p in bar)
                     = Kp_boud  * p_CO^2 / p_CO2
               with Kp_crack = 3.2283 and Kp_boud = 5.5887 at 900 K -- both
               give 1.580, because reforming and the shift are the complete
               set for the five gases, so the gas is fully equilibrated.
  Raise the steam to S/C 3.0 and a_C falls to 0.294: carbon is absent
  everywhere and the three reactors agree -- the declared list then costs
  nothing.

  NOT here, and said: any rate (whisker and encapsulating carbon and their
  kinetics are not modelled), a second solid, a solid in the feed (refused
  by name), non-ideal fugacities, and any reactor that is not isothermal.
  Record: docs/design/a-solid-in-a-declared-reaction-set.md.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

units
(
    {
        name        split;
        type        splitter;
        in          feed;
        outputs     ( feedGibbs feedDeclared feedComplete );
        operation   { fractions ( 0.5 0.25 0.25 ); }
    }
    {
        name        gibbs;
        type        gibbsReactor;
        in          feedGibbs;
        outputs     ( outGibbs );
        operation
        {
            T          900 K;
            P          1 bar;
            elements   ( C  H  O );
            species
            (
                { name  CH4;       atoms  ( 1  4  0 ); }
                { name  water;     atoms  ( 0  2  1 ); }
                { name  CO;        atoms  ( 1  0  1 ); }
                { name  CO2;       atoms  ( 1  0  2 ); }
                { name  H2;        atoms  ( 0  2  0 ); }
                { name  graphite;  atoms  ( 1  0  0 ); }
            );
        }
    }
    {
        name        declared;
        type        equilibriumReactor;
        in          feedDeclared;
        outputs     ( outDeclared );
        operation
        {
            T          900 K;
        }
        reactions   ( steamReforming waterGasShift );   // Boudouard and cracking EXCLUDED
    }
    {
        name        complete;
        type        equilibriumReactor;
        in          feedComplete;
        outputs     ( outComplete );
        operation
        {
            T          900 K;
        }
        reactions   ( steamReforming waterGasShift methaneCracking );
    }
);
